---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/intermediate/christianity">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate lesson in Religious Studies. It assumes the foundational lesson
    The World's Traditions, which introduced the major faiths from the outside — their
    numbers, founders and family resemblances — and the academic habit of treating a
    tradition's own categories with precision. Here we go inside one tradition: the claims,
    the metaphysics, the book, the splits and the practice, always distinguishing what
    historians can establish from what believers profess.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state the Trinity's precise grammar and explain which rejected alternatives defined it;</li>
    <li>separate the historical, faith and doctrinal layers of claims about Jesus, from the execution under Pilate to Chalcedon's two natures;</li>
    <li>explain how the biblical canon actually formed, and why the Nicaea story is wrong;</li>
    <li>account for the great schisms of 1054 and 1517 as doctrine carrying politics;</li>
    <li>describe the sacraments, church structures and liturgical calendar — and say why Easter, not Christmas, is the tradition's centre.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Christianity is the world's largest religion — roughly 2.4 billion people,
    about a third of humanity — <strong>founded on the resurrection claim,
    organised around scripture and sacrament, and split historically into
    Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant branches</strong>. Its 2,000-year arc runs
    from a persecuted Jewish sect to the most geographically distributed
    tradition on Earth, and the study's constant is translation: how one message
    — about a Jewish teacher executed under Pontius Pilate and proclaimed risen —
    was re-expressed in Greek metaphysics, Latin law, and every culture it
    entered since.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the claim, the ascent, the schisms, and the canon" widget="equationStep" config={christCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four stages and treat each as a thread
    the sections below unravel. Step 1 is the load-bearing claim — everything downstream
    presupposes a stance toward it. Step 2 is the sociological turn: a persecuted sect becomes
    the establishment, with consequences for creeds, canon and basilicas. Step 3 shows each
    schism carrying national politics on doctrinal back; step 4 points at the book and the
    creeds — the subject of the Bible section's misconception about Nicaea.
  </p>

  <h2>Beliefs about God: the Trinity and its grammar</h2>
  <p>
    Christian claims about God are Jewish inheritance plus a distinctive
    development. God is one (the Shema stands) — creator of everything from
    nothing (<em>ex nihilo</em>), omnipotent, omniscient, perfectly good —
    and yet known as <strong>Trinity</strong>: one divine essence, three
    <em>persons</em> (Father, Son, Holy Spirit), distinguished by relationship
    (the Father begetting, the Son begotten, the Spirit proceeding) without
    division of being. The formulation is the tradition's most distinctive and
    most argued metaphysics, and the great councils were largely grammatical
    emergencies about it: if Jesus is worshipped, and there is one God, then
    what is Jesus? The answers that lost became named as heresies and are
    instructive on every side — <strong>Arianism</strong> (the Son is a created
    being: solves monotheism, ends worship of Christ — answered by Nicaea 325's
    <em>homoousios</em>, "of the same substance"), <strong>Modalism</strong>
    (the persons are masks of one actor: keeps monotheism, dissolves the
    relationships), <strong>tritheism</strong> (three gods: keeps threeness,
    ends the Shema). The doctrine's logic is that salvation requires God's own
    involvement — nothing less than God could reunite humanity to God — so
    Christ <em>must</em> be divine, and the Spirit (who dwells in believers)
    must be too.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each rejected answer with what it buys — and what it breaks" widget="match" config={christHeresyMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the Trinity teaches three separate gods. The doctrine's precise grammar — one essence, three persons — was constructed precisely to avoid that conclusion; the historic label for "three gods" is the heresy of tritheism, and the persons are distinguished by relations of origin, not by division of being. Judging the doctrine from its cartoon version is how the tradition's most technical claim gets misjudged most often.</p>

  <h2>Beliefs about Jesus: history, incarnation, atonement</h2>
  <p>
    Three layers of claim about Jesus of Nazareth (c. 4 BCE–30/33 CE) must be
    kept distinct. The <strong>historical</strong> layer: a Galilean Jew,
    baptised by John, teaching the imminent kingdom of God in parables,
    healing, gathering disciples, executed by crucifixion under the prefect
    Pontius Pilate — attested by the gospels and confirmed by the Roman
    historian Tacitus, which makes the execution itself among the best-attested
    facts of ancient religion. The <strong>faith</strong> layer: his followers
    proclaimed that God raised him — the resurrection claim that founded the
    movement (Paul's letters, written in the 50s and the earliest Christian
    documents, already assume it), and the confession that this man was the
    <strong>Messiah</strong> (Christ, "anointed" — a Jewish hope the tradition
    re-read through his life and death), <em>Son of God</em>, and in John's
    Gospel the eternal <em>Logos</em> made flesh. The <strong>doctrinal</strong>
    layer: the Council of <strong>Chalcedon</strong> (451) fixed the paradox as
    doctrine — one person, "fully God and fully human", two natures without
    confusion or division — against a century of alternatives (human Jesus
    adopted by God; divine Christ appearing merely human). <strong>Atonement</strong>
    — how the death "works" — has never been single: <em>Christus victor</em>
    (the early church: Christ's death-resurrection defeats death and the
    powers), <em>satisfaction</em> and <em>penal substitution</em> (Anselm and
    the Reformers: the debt or penalty of sin paid — the evangelical default),
    <em>moral influence</em> (the cross as love that transforms), each with
    scripture behind it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each claim into its layer" widget="sort-buckets" config={christLayersSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the Father is inherited from Israel's Shema, and the Son's status
    took the councils four centuries to fix in grammar. The creed names a third person that
    everyday religion tends to reduce to a feeling — the next section gives it its doctrine
    and its history back.
  </p>

  <h2>The Holy Spirit: the quiet person of the creed</h2>
  <p>
    The Spirit (<em>Pneuma</em>, breath) is the Trinity's third person and the
    one most easily reduced to a feeling. Doctrine gives it work: present at
    creation (brooding over the waters), inspiring the prophets and the
    scriptures, descending at <strong>Pentecost</strong> (Acts 2: wind, fire,
    tongues — the church's birthday) and ever after as the church's life —
    the one who sanctifies, guides ("it seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to
    us", the apostles' council says), and makes the sacraments effective.
    The Spirit's <em>procession</em> from the Father "and the Son"
    (<strong>filioque</strong>) — a single Latin word added unilaterally to the
    creed in the West — was a genuine theological dispute about the Trinity's
    inner relations, and one of the official triggers of the 1054 schism: a
    reminder that in this tradition, grammar is theology. In practice the
    Spirit is where Christianity meets experience: mystics, revivalism, and
    above all <strong>Pentecostalism</strong> (from the Azusa Street revival,
    1906) — Spirit-baptism, tongues, healing — now the fastest-growing
    Christian movement on Earth, and the strongest evidence that the doctrine's
    emphasis tracks the tradition's lived centre.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="order the Spirit's work, as doctrine tells it" widget="order" config={christSpiritOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one God in three persons, one Christ in two natures — and one
    added Latin word, filioque, that helped split East from West: in this tradition, grammar
    is theology. The next section turns to the book both halves read, and to how its contents
    were actually settled.
  </p>

  <h2>The Bible: canon and interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    The Christian Bible is a two-testament library. The <strong>Old
    Testament</strong> is Israel's scripture, received through the Greek
    <em>Septuagint</em> translation — which is why Catholic and Orthodox Bibles
    include the deuterocanonical books (Tobit, Maccabees, Wisdom — composed in
    Greek) while Protestant Bibles, following the Hebrew canon, relegate them
    to "apocrypha": an early sign that canon and community define each other.
    The <strong>New Testament</strong> (27 books) grew in order: Paul's letters
    (50s CE — earliest), then the gospels (Mark around 70 CE; Matthew and Luke
    drawing on Mark plus a lost sayings source, the "Q" hypothesis; John last,
    c. 90–100, the most theological), then the rest — with the list settled
    only in the fourth century (Athanasius' Easter letter of 367 is the first
    exactly modern list), by consensus of use rather than decree. The
    <strong>interpretation</strong> question — what kind of book is this? —
    divides the branches more than any doctrine. The ancient answers were
    plural by design: literal plus allegorical (Origen), later codified as the
    medieval <em>fourfold sense</em> (literal, allegorical, moral,
    anagogical). Modernity added the <strong>historical-critical method</strong>
    — reading the texts as documents with dates, sources and agendas, which
    changed theology itself (the "quest for the historical Jesus") — and the
    denominations took different settlements: Catholic teaching holds scripture
    interpreted within tradition under the magisterium; Orthodox treat
    scripture as the church's living book within liturgy; Protestantism
    (Luther's <em>sola scriptura</em>) made scripture the final authority
    alone — which fragmented as predictably as it liberated, and whose spectrum
    runs from strict inerrantist literalism to fully critical scholarship
    inside the same adjective.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="place the first exactly modern list" widget="place" config={christCanonPlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the Emperor Constantine chose the Bible's books at the Council of Nicaea. Nicaea (325) debated the divinity of Christ, not the canon; the New Testament's 27-book list emerged by consensus of use across churches and first appears in exactly modern form in Athanasius' 367 Easter letter — decades after Constantine. Canon-making was gradual, liturgical and decentralised: a library the church recognised, not a shelf a committee assembled.</p>

  <h2>From sect to empire — and the great schisms</h2>
  <p>
    The movement's first decisive turn was sociological: Paul's missions took
    it to Gentiles, and the <strong>Council of Jerusalem</strong> (c. 50 CE)
    decided converts need not keep the Torah — circumcision, kashrut, Sabbath —
    the moment a Jewish sect began becoming a world religion. Constantine
    legalised Christianity (313) and Theodosius made it the empire's official
    religion (380): the persecuted became the establishment, and the
    establishment built the creeds, the canon and the great basilicas.
    <strong>Augustine</strong> (354–430) supplied the West's software — original
    sin, grace, the just-war theory — a synthesis whose defaults still run
    secular Western thought. Then the splits. <strong>1054</strong>: East and
    West divided (Orthodox and Catholic) over papal authority, the filioque,
    and mutual excommunications — dogma carrying politics. <strong>1517</strong>:
    Luther's protest (indulgences were the spark; authority and salvation by
    faith were the engine) produced the Reformation and Protestantism's
    long fragmentation — every nation a church, every dispute a denomination —
    plus the Catholic Counter-Reformation (Trent) and a century of religious
    wars that taught Europe disestablishment the hard way.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="scrub the arc from sect to schism" widget="timeline" config={christArcTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the arc from persecuted sect to establishment produced the
    creeds, the canon and two great schisms — each split doctrinal <em>and</em> political.
    The next section moves from belief to lived tradition: sacraments, church structures and
    a calendar that rehearses the faith's claims in time.
  </p>

  <h2>Practice: sacraments, church, and sacred time</h2>
  <p>
    The lived tradition runs on <strong>sacraments</strong> — outward signs
    believed to convey inward grace. <strong>Baptism</strong> (water, in the
    name of the Trinity) is entry — administered to infants in most branches,
    to confessing adults in the Baptist and Pentecostal worlds. The
    <strong>Eucharist</strong> (Lord's Supper, Mass, Communion) is the centre
    — bread and wine re-enacting the last supper — and its interpretation
    split the churches as decisively as any creed: Catholic doctrine of
    <em>transubstantiation</em> (the substances truly become Christ's body
    and blood, appearances aside), Lutheran real presence, the Reformed
    spiritual-presence view, the memorialist reading — a single meal, four
    theologies, all of them arguments about what "this is my body" means.
    Church structure diverges with it: the Catholic Church's papacy and
    magisterium, the Orthodox conciliar episcopacy, the Protestant pastoral
    and congregational forms. <strong>Monasticism</strong> — from the desert
    fathers through Benedict's sixth-century rule — became the engine of
    medieval Christianity: preserved classical learning, ran the schools and
    hospitals, and set the rhythm (prayer seven times a day) that the
    liturgical <strong>year</strong> also keeps: Advent, Christmas, Lent's
    forty-day fast, Holy Week, <strong>Easter</strong> (the tradition's true
    centre, theologically outweighing Christmas), and Pentecost — a calendar
    that rehearses the faith's claims in time.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="one meal, four readings — step into each" widget="branch" config={christEucharistBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Christmas is Christianity's most important festival. Theologically the year's centre of gravity is Easter — the resurrection claim is the faith's foundation, and every liturgical calendar treats the Easter season, not Christmas, as its summit. Christmas grew into its popular cultural weight late, its December date and customs being medieval and Victorian accumulations, which is why the church's own ranking places it below the Paschal feast.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="drag between the two feasts" widget="compare" config={christFeastsCompare} />

  <h2>The branches today</h2>
  <p>
    Of the world's roughly 2.4 billion Christians (Pew's proportions), about
    half are <strong>Catholic</strong> (~1.3 billion, the largest single
    organisation in human history, led by the Bishop of Rome), roughly a third
    <strong>Protestant</strong> — itself a thousand-headed family from
    Lutherans and Anglicans (a via-media between Catholic and Protestant) to
    Baptists, Methodists, Pentecostals and the African Independent Churches —
    and about one in eight <strong>Orthodox</strong> (~260 million, organised
    in national churches — Russian, Greek, Serbian, Ethiopian — with the
    Ecumenical Patriarch as first-among-equals rather than pope). The
    <strong>Oriental Orthodox</strong> (Armenian, Coptic, Ethiopian —
    separated since 451 over Chalcedon's wording) and the ancient <strong>Church
    of the East</strong> are reminders that the schism map is older and
    stranger than the three-branch cartoon. One honest footnote: Christian
    mission and European colonialism were entangled for four centuries — the
    mission stations brought schools, hospitals and literacy <em>and</em>
    cultural subjugation, which is why the postcolonial churches' independence
    movements (the African Independent Churches, Korean Christianity's
    national character, liberation theology) are not footnotes to the story
    but its current centre of gravity.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the faulty line — the branch survey" widget="spot-error" config={christBranchSpot} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the Trinity's precise grammar, and name the historic heresy that says "three gods".</li>
    <li>A friend claims the Emperor Constantine chose the Bible's books at the Council of Nicaea. Give the two-sentence correction.</li>
    <li>Why is Easter, not Christmas, the theological centre of the Christian year?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) One divine essence, three <em>persons</em> — Father, Son and
    Holy Spirit — distinguished by relations of origin, not by division of being. The historic
    label for "three gods" is <em>tritheism</em>; the doctrine's grammar was constructed
    precisely to rule that out, along with Arianism (which ends worship of Christ) and
    Modalism (which dissolves the relationships). (2) Nicaea (325) debated the divinity of
    Christ, not the canon; the New Testament's 27 books emerged by consensus of use across
    churches, first appearing in exactly modern form in Athanasius' Easter letter of 367 —
    decades after Constantine. Canon-making was gradual, liturgical and decentralised. (3)
    Because the resurrection claim is the faith's foundation — the proclamation that founded
    the movement — and the liturgical calendar treats the Easter season as its summit.
    Christmas's popular cultural weight is a late, medieval and Victorian accumulation; the
    church's own ranking places it below the Paschal feast.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Christianity is founded on the resurrection claim, organised around scripture and sacrament, and split into Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant branches.</li>
    <li>Trinity: one essence, three persons; the rejected alternatives — Arianism, Modalism, tritheism — show what each part of the grammar protects.</li>
    <li>Claims about Jesus come in three layers: historical (execution under Pilate, well attested), faith (resurrection, Messiah), doctrinal (Chalcedon's "fully God and fully human").</li>
    <li>Atonement has never been single doctrine: Christus victor, satisfaction and penal substitution, and moral influence each have scripture behind them.</li>
    <li>The filioque clause and papal authority drove the 1054 schism; Luther's protest drove 1517 — in both, dogma carried politics.</li>
    <li>The canon grew by consensus of use, settled only in the fourth century; Nicaea did not choose the books.</li>
    <li>Practice runs on sacraments — baptism and a Eucharist with four theologies of one meal — on a calendar whose summit is Easter, and today on Pentecostalism, the fastest-growing movement.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson Judaism deepens the inheritance this page keeps invoking — the Shema,
    the Messiah hope, Israel's scripture that Christians call the Old Testament — and
    Studying Religion Academically examines the method this lesson used: separating
    historical evidence from insider claims without dismissing either. Islam, the third
    Abrahamic sibling, takes the same one-God inheritance in a direction Christianity's
    councils never faced.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four extensions. <strong>Eschatology</strong> — the tradition's future tense
    — has cycled through Kingdom-realised (Christ's return expected within
    decades), amillennial (symbolic), and recurring apocalyptic movements (the
    Millerites; today's dispensationalism with its Rapture reading, a
    nineteenth-century innovation mistaken by outsiders for the tradition's
    core). <strong>Christianity and science</strong> is neither the cartoon war
    nor the cartoon harmony: universities, hospitals and the scientific
    revolution's early personnel were substantially Christian; Galileo's
    condemnation (1633) was real; Darwin's reception split by denomination and
    nation; the "warfare" thesis of the 1890s is itself historical scholarship
    now considered dated. <strong>The global shift</strong>: Christianity's
    centre of gravity moved south — most Christians now live in Africa, Latin
    America and Asia, with Pentecostalism the engine — so the "Western"
    religion is no longer Western in composition, and the theologies of
    liberation (Latin America, 1970s: God's "preferential option for the
    poor"), African indigenous Christianity and Korean megachurches are not
    variants but increasingly the mainstream. <strong>Ecumenism</strong> — the
    uniting counter-movement of the twentieth century (the World Council of
    Churches, 1948; the 1999 Catholic–Lutheran Joint Declaration on
    justification that settled Luther's original dispute) healed much
    vocabulary — while the sexuality disputes of the present split Protestants
    anew. And the <strong>open
    questions</strong>: Christian–Jewish relations after the Holocaust
    (supersessionism's formal renunciation by the Catholic Church, Nostra
    Aetate 1965, was epochal); women's ordination and sexuality, the live
    communion-splitting disputes of the present; and the oldest one, still
    running: how much may the faith's form change before the faith has
    changed?
  </p>
